// Title: Potential Persistence Via LSA Extensions
// Sigma ID: 41f6531d-af6e-4c6e-918f-b946f2b85a36
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: Nasreddine Bencherchali (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2022-07-21 | Modified: 2023-08-17
// ATT&CK: persistence
// Description: Detects when an attacker modifies the "REG_MULTI_SZ" value named "Extensions" to include a
//   custom DLL to achieve persistence via lsass. The "Extensions" list contains filenames of DLLs
//   being automatically loaded by lsass.exe. Each DLL has its InitializeLsaExtension() method called
//   after loading.
// False positives:
//   - Unlikely
// References:
//   - https://persistence-info.github.io/Data/lsaaextension.html
//   - https://twitter.com/0gtweet/status/1476286368385019906
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/ccbb0369507f796db0b0ae6854c8a2be471b9d84/rules/windows/registry/registry_set/registry_set_persistence_lsa_extension.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where RegistryKey contains "\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\LsaExtensionConfig\\LsaSrv\\Extensions"
